#v(0.3in)

Choose one or two activities from the options below that match your interests. Each activity can be completed in approximately 60--90 minutes. All activities run entirely in your web browser, with nothing to install. Most do not require an account; where one is needed (or recommended), this is noted.

#v(0.2in)

== Category 1: Logic Puzzles & Programming Foundations

These activities focus on core programming concepts through engaging puzzle-solving.

=== Lightbot
*What you'll learn:* Sequences, loops, conditionals, and procedures

*How it works:* Guide a robot to light up tiles on a grid using programming logic. Start with simple commands and work up to complex challenges involving functions and loops.

*Time:* 45--60 minutes for the core levels

*Best for:* Visual learners who enjoy puzzle-solving; no coding syntax required

*Account needed:* No

*Access:* https://lightbot.lu/#/welcome (browser version, runs directly, no install or login)

---

=== Blockly Games
*What you'll learn:* Sequencing, loops, conditionals, functions, and a gentle transition to real JavaScript

*How it works:* A series of progressively harder block-based puzzles from Google. Start with Maze and Bird (basic sequencing and conditionals), then move to Turtle (drawing with code) and Pond (competitive programming with real code). Later levels reveal the equivalent JavaScript code for what you built with blocks.

*Time:* 60--90 minutes (work through 2--4 games)

*Best for:* Beginners who want a structured, scaffolded introduction; students who enjoy seeing how visual blocks map to text-based code

*Account needed:* No

*Access:* https://blockly.games/
---

=== CodinGame Puzzles
*What you'll learn:* Problem-solving, algorithms, and coding in your language of choice

*How it works:* Solve self-contained coding challenges ranging from easy to hard, using a real code editor in the browser. CodinGame has dozens of interactive puzzles covering a wide variety of programming concepts.

*Time:* 60--90 minutes (choose 3--5 puzzles)

*Best for:* Students with coding experience who want to sharpen problem-solving skills; supports 25+ languages

*Account needed:* No for trying puzzles in the editor; an account is only needed to save progress

*Access:* codingame.com/start

---

== Category 2: Game-Based Coding Adventures

These platforms let you code real programs while progressing through a storyline or game.

=== Scratch (Online Editor)
*What you'll learn:* Visual block-based programming; game design; animation; storytelling

*How it works:* Create interactive games, animations, or stories using drag-and-drop blocks, directly in the browser-based editor. Thousands of project ideas and tutorials are available to remix or follow along with.

*Time:* 60--90 minutes (create a small game or interactive story)

*Best for:* Students who love creative projects and game design; good introduction to programming logic without syntax

*Account needed:* No to use the editor and experiment; an account is only needed to save your project or share it with the community

*Access:* scratch.mit.edu/projects/editor

