== Category 3: Quick Skill Challenges

Perfect for students who want intense, focused problem-solving in the browser.

=== Exercism (Try Mode)
*What you'll learn:* Algorithm design, syntax practice, and idiomatic code in a language of your choice

*How it works:* Short, focused coding exercises with an in-browser editor and automatic test feedback. Exercism's "Try" mode lets you attempt exercises directly in the browser without setting anything up.

*Time:* 60--90 minutes (complete several small exercises)

*Best for:* Students with some coding experience who want quick, varied practice across many small problems

*Account needed:* No for trying exercises in the browser; an account is only needed to track progress and unlock the full syllabus

*Access:* exercism.org

---

=== CodinGame Practice Puzzles
*What you'll learn:* Algorithms, data structures, and general problem-solving

*How it works:* Browse the puzzle library by difficulty and topic, and solve directly in the in-browser code editor with instant test feedback.

*Time:* 60--90 minutes (choose 3--5 puzzles at your level)

*Best for:* Students who enjoy algorithmic thinking and want a large variety of self-contained challenges

*Account needed:* No to attempt puzzles; an account is only needed to save solutions and track stats

*Access:* codingame.com/training

---

== Category 4: Artificial Intelligence & Machine Learning

Hands-on AI training in multiple styles, from visual/interactive to data-driven.

=== Google Teachable Machine
*What you'll learn:* Training data, model classification, pattern recognition

*How it works:* Train an AI model using your webcam, microphone, or uploaded images, directly in the browser. Teach the computer to recognize patterns (gestures, sounds, objects) by providing examples.

*Time:* 45--60 minutes to build a complete project

*Best for:* Visual learners; creative students; those who like immediate feedback

*Account needed:* No

*Example projects:*
- Hand gesture recognition game controller
- Sound classifier (detect different claps, snaps, whistles)
- Custom emoji generator based on facial expressions

*Access:* teachablemachine.withgoogle.com

---

=== TensorFlow Playground
*What you'll learn:* How neural networks work, activation functions, learning rates, overfitting

*How it works:* Play with a real neural network in your browser. Adjust the number of hidden layers, neurons, activation functions, and learning rates while watching the network train in real time on different datasets.

*Time:* 60--90 minutes (explore multiple configurations and datasets)

*Best for:* Students curious about "what's inside" AI; visual learners; those interested in the mechanics of deep learning

*Account needed:* No

*Key insights:* Understand concepts like overfitting, underfitting, and decision boundaries without any math

*Access:* playground.tensorflow.org

---

=== Machine Learning for Kids (Decision Tree Visualization)
*What you'll learn:* How machine learning models make decisions, training data, classification logic

*How it works:* Create a classification model by collecting examples (play games or draw), then see an interactive visualization of the decision tree your data created. Follow the tree branches to understand exactly how the model makes predictions.

*Time:* 45--75 minutes per activity

*Best for:* Visual/spatial learners; students who like understanding the "why" behind predictions; those interested in interpretable AI

*Account needed:* Yes (free account required to train and save models)

*Example activities:*
- Train a model to recognize sprites in Scratch (Pac-Man vs. ghost)
- Classify numbers or simple objects
- Play Noughts and Crosses to train a game strategy model

*Access:* machinelearningforkids.co.uk

---

=== R2D3 Visual Introduction to Machine Learning
*What you'll learn:* Decision trees, overfitting vs. underfitting, training vs. test data, the bias-variance tradeoff

*How it works:* Interactive storytelling that visualizes ML concepts step by step, directly in the browser. No training or code required, just follow the narrative and interact with the visualizations to build intuition about how models learn.

*Time:* 60--90 minutes (complete both parts)

*Best for:* Conceptual learners; students who appreciate beautiful data visualization; those wanting to understand fundamental ML concepts

*Account needed:* No

*Key concept:* Explores why a "perfect" model on training data might fail on new data

*Access:* r2d3.us/visual-intro-to-machine-learning-part-1

---

=== AI Literacy Activities (Code.org)
*What you'll learn:* AI concepts, bias in machine learning, practical AI applications, societal impacts

*How it works:* Guided tutorials and hands-on activities, run in the browser. Learn about how AI models make decisions, bias in training data, and real-world consequences.

*Time:* 60 minutes per activity (over 100 free options available)

*Best for:* Students interested in the societal impact of AI; ethical considerations; AI literacy

*Account needed:* No for most activities (some progress-saving features require a login)

*Access:* code.org/ai

---

== Category 5: Web Development & Creative Coding

Build something visible that you can share, all in the browser.

=== Khan Academy (JavaScript)
*What you'll learn:* JavaScript fundamentals; drawing and animation; interactive programs

*How it works:* Short video lessons followed by coding challenges in an in-browser editor. Draw shapes, create animations, build simple games, and see your code run instantly alongside the editor.

*Time:* 60--90 minutes (choose a project-based unit)

*Best for:* Structured learners who like video explanations; visual/creative output

*Account needed:* No to use the editor and follow lessons; an account is only needed to save progress

*Access:* khanacademy.org/computing/computer-programming

---

=== p5.js Web Editor
*What you'll learn:* JavaScript fundamentals through creative coding: drawing, animation, interactivity, and simple games

*How it works:* A friendly, browser-based code editor designed for visual and creative projects. Write a few lines of code and immediately see shapes, colors, and animations appear. Comes with a large library of beginner-friendly examples to start from.

*Time:* 60--90 minutes (start from an example and modify it into your own creation)

*Best for:* Creative students; those who enjoy seeing immediate visual results from their code

*Account needed:* No to use the editor; an account is only needed to save sketches to the cloud

*Access:* editor.p5js.org

---

== Quick Selection Guide

*Choose based on your interests:*

- *Love puzzles?* → Lightbot or Blockly Games
- *Want to code an adventure or creative project?* → Scratch or p5.js Web Editor
- *Like competition & time pressure?* → Clash of Code
- *Want to understand how neural networks work?* → TensorFlow Playground
- *Fascinated by AI & want to train a model?* → Google Teachable Machine
- *Want to understand model decision-making?* → Machine Learning for Kids or R2D3
- *Interested in AI ethics & impact?* → AI Literacy (Code.org)
- *Have coding experience & want quick challenges?* → CodinGame or Exercism
- *Prefer structured video lessons?* → Khan Academy

== Tips for Your 90-Minute Session

1. *Start with something fun, not frustrating.* Choose an activity that matches your current skill level.

2. *Expect to get stuck.* That's normal! Debugging and problem-solving are core CS skills. Use that frustration as motivation.

3. *Read error messages carefully.* They often tell you exactly what went wrong.

4. *Take breaks* if you hit a wall. Sometimes stepping away helps you see the solution.

5. *Explore the community.* Most platforms have discussion forums, tutorials, and walkthroughs. Don't hesitate to read hints.

6. *Share your results.* Some of these platforms let you share what you built. Show your classmates what you created!

